  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 114 PM CDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nStrong southerly flow remains in place through Sunday as a jet max \ntracks by to the north along the Canadian border. Gusts of 35 to 45 \nmph will be possible along the Buffalo Ridge tonight, with the \nbetter chances for the higher winds this evening into the early \novernight hours. This will bring milder lows tonight, generally in \nthe upper 30s to mid 40s, with highs Sunday warming into the mid 60s \nto lower 70s, warmest west of the James. As this jet max shifts east \nof the area, a weak front will sag into the area and will help to \ndiminish the stronger southerly winds. This will likely lead to more \nlows on Monday morning in the upper 30s, but not as cold as this \nmorning. With a somewhat slow recovery to southerly flow \ntemperatures will be a touch cooler and close to the seasonal \nnormals in the lower 60s.\n\nMonday night into Tuesday southerly flow increases and brings warmer \nreadings back into the area. Highs will generally be 65 to 70 \ndegrees. \n\nA quick moving trough will swing through Tuesday night into \nWednesday and bring a very small chance for very light rainfall \namounts. For now will leave any mention out of the forecast, but the \nslightly better chances will be in northwest IA. \n\nVery mild air will build into the area Wednesday into Saturday with \nhighs very likely in the upper 60s to mid 70s each day. With strong \nsoutherly flow on a few of the days overnight lows will be in the \nmid to upper 50s, about 15 to 20 degrees above normal. Will need to \nkeep an eye on the fire weather potential late week into the weekend \nas fuels continue to cure and harvesting activities peak.",
